WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.937
Web and API input should be validated on the server before business logic
acts on it.

2
00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:12.485
Request bodies, path parameters, query parameters, headers, cookies, form
fields, and file metadata all need expectations.

3
00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:18.057
Frameworks can help, but framework defaults are not the same as a complete
validation design.

4
00:00:18.057 --> 00:00:22.712
The application should define what each endpoint accepts and what it
rejects.

5
00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:27.790
Flask applications often require explicit request parsing and validation
choices.

6
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:33.363
Django provides forms, model forms, serializers in common API stacks, and
validation hooks.

7
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:38.159
FastAPI commonly uses Pydantic models for request bodies and typed
parameters.

8
00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:47.893
Each framework offers tools, but the developer is still responsible for
applying them consistently and enforcing business rules that the framework
cannot infer.

9
00:00:47.893 --> 00:00:50.502
File metadata deserves special attention.

10
00:00:50.502 --> 00:00:57.062
A filename, extension, content type, declared size, and form field may all
be user-controlled or misleading.

11
00:00:57.062 --> 00:01:04.962
A server should validate file size, expected type, allowed use, storage
location, and how the file will later be served or processed.

12
00:01:04.962 --> 00:01:09.617
Validation should not depend on the client telling the truth about the
upload.

13
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:14.837
Validation error messages should be useful without revealing unnecessary
internals.

14
00:01:14.837 --> 00:01:19.774
A response can say that a field is required, out of range, malformed, or
not allowed.

15
00:01:19.774 --> 00:01:28.802
It should not expose stack traces, secret values, database structure,
parser internals, or implementation details that do not help a legitimate
user.

16
00:01:28.802 --> 00:01:35.080
Good API validation gives clear feedback while keeping sensitive
information out of responses and logs.
